  "text_sample": "Your Page Title Williamsburg \u00ae Shop Journal About Account Overview Cart ( 0 ) Cart ( 0 ) Menu Close Departments All boots (20) Wedge sole (16) Lug sole (04) New arrivals Bedford moc toe Bedford moc toe Wythe moc toe Bedford moc toe (001) 2026 Wythe moc toe (002) 2026 Shop Journal About Account Overview Wedge sole (16) Lug sole (04) Inside a Goodyear welt How a strip of leather holds a boot together, and why it means you can resole it for decades. All notes Ingrid Solberg September 12, 2026, 1 minute read Two stitches, one strip A welt is a strip of leather that runs around the edge of the boot. One stitch joins it to the upper, a second joins it to the sole. Nothing is glued that has to hold. What it gives you Because the sole is stitched to the welt and not to the upper, it can come off without touching the rest of the boot. That is what makes a resole possible, again and again. What it asks of you A welted boot is stiff for the first weeks. The cork under your foot moulds to your step, and after a month the boot is yours and nobody else\u2019s. More from the journal All notes Matteo Ferraro, September 28, 2026 Why we make one boot Erik Lindqvist, August 30, 2026 A week breaking in a n",
